boom! I think there are other reasons may be that&nbsp;
you remember names. But the point is in order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.960
these 3 key ideas that I'm going to share on&nbsp;
how to say the right things in conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.800
you have to actually care about the people you're&nbsp;
talking to. You can't fudge this. You can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.520
manufacture and just say the right things.&nbsp;
Because part of the idea of saying the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.040
things in conversation is being as authentic&nbsp;
as possible in that conversation. And so, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.280
you're putting on some sort of face and trying to&nbsp;
be something or trying to do something else, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.640
not going to click or resonate with that person.&nbsp;
And so, in order for these to work, you've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:04.960
care as Dominic said he did. The idea number one&nbsp;
is reflectively listen. Reflective listening is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.560
an extremely simple and underutilized tool. But&nbsp;
it's phenomenally useful because it allows you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.200
to just hold up a mirror to the other person and&nbsp;
reflect back what they're saying. And so, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.120
want to say the right things in conversation,&nbsp;
don't try to come up with that yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.320
Use the person's words, the person's sentiment,&nbsp;
etc. You can check out this other video with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.360
a whole breakdown of four different levels&nbsp;
of reflective listening and how to do them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:38.240
But in this video, just the short bit is hear what&nbsp;
somebody's saying, not just their words but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.760
music behind the words, the sentiment, what you're&nbsp;
noticing, what you're observing and just reflect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:49.440
that back. You can reflect it back verbatim what&nbsp;
they said or simply translate what they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.800
into your own words and feed it back to them.&nbsp;
It is a phenomenal what happens in our brains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.960
when we're fed back words or ideas that we&nbsp;
shared but didn't even really process until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.200
somebody spoke them back to us. In the same way&nbsp;
that you don't know that you have a booger or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.040
raisin in your tooth until you walk into a mirror&nbsp;
and you see that for yourself. And when you gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.680
that awareness of what you're saying and what&nbsp;
you're doing, it's oftentimes the exact right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:22.960
thing to happen in a conversation. Key idea&nbsp;
number two is turn assumptions into questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.680
right? Assumptions are not fundamentally bad. They&nbsp;
are so natural. It's really useful that we make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.800
assumptions oftentimes. But the key to saying the&nbsp;
right thing in conversation is to not act on those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.000
assumptions or speak as if those assumptions were&nbsp;
true. So, to say the right thing in a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.320
if you do have an assumption, recognize that&nbsp;
an assumption is just a story that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:49.280
and then turn that assumption into a&nbsp;
question not rooted in any judgment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.680
ask it. Sometimes when you're trying to figure&nbsp;
out the right thing to say in a conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.240
it's a question to unpack and further dive into&nbsp;
the conversation. When you're thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.760
how to say the right thing in a conversation, you&nbsp;
don't want to be really concerned with what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.520
saying out of this device. You want to be more&nbsp;
concerned with how you're using these 2 appendages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.400
on the side of your head and being deliberate&nbsp;
in the questions that you're asking. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.800
people are rarely asked questions based on what&nbsp;
they've just said. So, often in conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.320
it goes sentence ends in a period; sentence it&nbsp;
ends in a period; sentence ends in a period. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.120
we break that mold and somebody says a sentence&nbsp;
that ends in a period and we fire back a question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.520
it feels great. Right? I haven't met a single&nbsp;
person on the planet that doesn't want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.760
seen heard and understood at some level. So,&nbsp;
it's really, really phenomenal to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.760
be asked about what we've just shared. The&nbsp;
third key idea here is to master empathy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

